<Layout title="Terms" description="Terms of use for the x402Interop marketplace." active="">
  <section class="phero">
    <div class="phero__inner">
      <h1>Terms of use.</h1>
      <p class="phero__lede">DRAFT for legal review.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="legal">
    <dl class="legal__facts">
      <div>
        <dt>Last updated</dt>
        <dd>10 November 2026</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Operator</dt>
        <dd>THE3RDWEBLABS</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Covers</dt>
        <dd>The x402Interop marketplace, including search, quotes, and listing management.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Questions</dt>
        <dd>
          <a href="https://github.com/the3rdweblabs/x402Interop">GitHub</a>
          {' · '}
          <a href="https://t.me/the3rdweblabs">Telegram</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2>1. Who these terms cover</h2>
    <p>
      These terms cover anyone who opens the marketplace. That includes someone browsing the
      catalogue, someone asking for a deposit quote, and someone who connects a wallet to list,
      edit, or remove a service. If you use the marketplace, you agree to this draft as it stands
      on the date above. The <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> explains what information the
      marketplace handles.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What the marketplace is</h2>
    <p>
      The marketplace is a directory. Sellers describe a service, a price, the networks they
      accept, and the address where they want to be paid. Buyers can search that directory and
      ask the facilitator for a deposit quote.
    </p>
    <p>
      THE3RDWEBLABS operates the directory and the facilitator. It does not sell the listed
      services, and it is not a party to a sale between a buyer and a seller. A listing is an
      offer from its owner. The endpoint behind a listing is run by that owner, or by whoever
      they point it at.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Wallets</h2>
    <p>
      Some actions need a wallet you control. Connecting a wallet shows its public address in the
      page. It does not give the marketplace your seed phrase or private key, and you should
      never type those into this site or send them to the operator.
    </p>
    <p>
      You are responsible for the wallet you connect, for the device it is on, and for every
      message or transaction you approve in it. If you lose access to the wallet that owns a
      listing, the marketplace cannot recover that listing for a different address.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Listings</h2>
    <p>
      If you list a service, you are responsible for the name, the description, the price, the
      networks, the payout address, and the endpoint. Those details should be accurate, and the
      endpoint should be one you are allowed to offer. Listings are free for now. If a listing
      fee is introduced, the amount and the asset will be shown before you are asked to pay it,
      and this page will be updated.
    </p>
    <p>Do not list a service that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>breaks the law where you offer it or where the buyer is likely to use it;</li>
      <li>facilitates fraud, phishing, malware, or unauthorised access;</li>
      <li>infringes someone else's intellectual property or other rights;</li>
      <li>exploits or sexualises a minor;</li>
      <li>impersonates another person, project, or operator.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The operator may refuse, hide, or remove a listing, and may stop an address from writing
      listings, when a listing breaks these rules or when a valid takedown notice arrives. Removing
      a listing from the directory does not reverse a payment that has already settled.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Quotes and payments</h2>
    <p>
      A deposit quote names one deposit address, one amount, and an expiry. It can be paid once.
      You send that amount from your own wallet to the deposit address before it expires. The
      payment moves on the networks named in the quote. The operator does not hold buyer funds
      for a listing and does not custody a seller's payout.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chain transfer is generally irreversible. The operator cannot reverse, refund, or cancel
      a payment that has been sent. A disagreement about whether a service was delivered, and any
      refund of that service, is between the buyer and the seller.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Signatures</h2>
    <p>
      Creating, editing, or removing a listing asks your wallet to sign a message. The message is
      tied to that one action and to a single-use challenge that expires after five minutes. The
      signature proves you control the address that owns the listing. It is not a transaction, and
      it does not authorise a transfer of funds. Read the message in your wallet before you sign it.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Services you do not operate</h2>
    <p>
      A listing points at an endpoint the marketplace does not run. Wallets, chain networks, and
      the cross-chain settlement path (NEAR Intents and 1Click) are separate services. Each has
      its own terms, its own availability, and its own risks. The marketplace does not promise
      that an endpoint will stay up, that a quote will still be fillable when you return to it,
      or that a third-party wallet or network will behave as you expect.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>Do not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>forge a signature, reuse a challenge, or present an address you do not control;</li>
      <li>probe, overload, or disrupt the marketplace or the facilitator;</li>
      <li>scrape the catalogue in a way that degrades it for other people;</li>
      <li>use the marketplace to send people to a harmful or deceptive endpoint.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. The site and your content</h2>
    <p>
      The marketplace software is published under the licence in the project repository. Text,
      names, and endpoints you submit in a listing stay yours. You give the operator permission
      to store that listing, show it in the catalogue, and remove it under these terms. That
      permission ends when the listing is removed, except for copies kept as part of a security
      log or a legal hold.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Availability and liability</h2>
    <p>
      The marketplace is provided as it is and as it is available. It can be interrupted for
      maintenance, a fault, or a dependency the operator does not control. To the fullest extent
      the law allows, the operator disclaims warranties of availability, fitness for a particular
      purpose, and non-infringement, and is not liable for indirect or consequential loss, for
      funds sent on a blockchain, or for what a buyer or a seller does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in these terms limits liability that the law does not allow to be limited, including
      liability for fraud, or for death or personal injury caused by negligence. The approved
      version of these terms will name any further cap that applies.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Changes, suspension, and the end of the service</h2>
    <p>
      The operator may update these terms, suspend the marketplace, or stop it. A change that
      affects how you list or pay will be posted on this page with a new date. If you keep using
      the marketplace after that date, you are using it under the updated terms. If the service
      stops, listings may no longer be shown. Payments that have already settled on a chain are
      not undone by the site going offline.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Governing law</h2>
    <p>
      The approved version of these terms will name the governing law and the courts that can
      hear a dispute. Until that version is published, these terms are a draft for review.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
